# Introduction
## Comparative Phylogenetic Reconstruction of Mammalian Evolution Using All 13 Mitochondrial Protein-Coding Genes

### 1. Background

Phylogenetics is a branch of evolutionary biology that investigates the evolutionary relationships among organisms by analyzing their inherited characteristics, including DNA, RNA, and protein sequences. Molecular phylogenetic analysis has become an important tool for understanding evolutionary history because genetic sequences provide measurable evidence of similarities and differences accumulated over generations. By comparing homologous sequences across species, researchers can estimate evolutionary relationships and investigate patterns of genetic conservation and divergence.

Mammalian evolution is characterized by substantial diversity in morphology, physiology, ecological adaptation, and genetic composition. Although many mammals share fundamental biological features, their genomes contain variations that can provide insights into their evolutionary relationships. Reconstructing these relationships using molecular data helps researchers investigate how different mammalian lineages are related and how genetic changes have accumulated during their evolutionary history.

Mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) is particularly useful in molecular phylogenetics because it is relatively compact, contains a defined set of protein-coding genes, and is available for a wide range of organisms. In mammals, the mitochondrial genome typically contains 37 genes, including 13 protein-coding genes, 22 transfer RNA genes, and two ribosomal RNA genes. The 13 protein-coding genes encode components of the oxidative phosphorylation system, which is essential for cellular energy production.

### 2. Significance of Mitochondrial Protein-Coding Genes

The 13 mitochondrial protein-coding genes are involved in the production of proteins required for the mitochondrial electron transport chain and ATP synthesis. These genes include seven NADH dehydrogenase subunits (ND1, ND2, ND3, ND4, ND4L, ND5, and ND6), three cytochrome c oxidase subunits (COX1, COX2, and COX3), cytochrome b (CYB), and two ATP synthase subunits (ATP6 and ATP8).

These genes exhibit different levels of sequence conservation and evolutionary variability. Some regions remain highly conserved because of functional constraints, whereas other regions accumulate substitutions at different rates. Consequently, individual mitochondrial genes may differ in their ability to distinguish closely related species or resolve deeper evolutionary relationships.

Analyzing only one mitochondrial gene may provide insufficient information for resolving certain evolutionary relationships. In contrast, combining information from all 13 protein-coding genes can provide a larger molecular dataset for phylogenetic reconstruction. However, a larger dataset does not automatically guarantee an accurate species tree. The effects of evolutionary rate variation, substitution models, sequence alignment, and shared mitochondrial inheritance must also be considered.

### 3. Phylogenetic Reconstruction and Computational Approaches

Computational phylogenetics uses mathematical and statistical methods to estimate evolutionary relationships from molecular sequence data. In this project, mitochondrial protein sequences from selected mammalian species will be collected from publicly available biological databases, particularly the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI).

The sequences will undergo quality assessment and multiple sequence alignment to identify corresponding amino acid positions across species. Individual gene alignments will then be used to investigate the evolutionary patterns of each mitochondrial protein. Pairwise evolutionary distances and phylogenetic trees will be calculated to examine the relationships inferred from different genes.

Distance-based methods, including Neighbor-Joining and UPGMA, will be considered alongside the Maximum Likelihood approach. Neighbor-Joining estimates a tree from evolutionary distance information, whereas UPGMA assumes a constant evolutionary rate across lineages. Maximum Likelihood estimates the tree that best explains the observed sequence data under a specified evolutionary model.

The project will also investigate a concatenated dataset generated by combining the alignments of all 13 mitochondrial protein-coding genes. Where appropriate, gene partitions and suitable amino acid substitution models will be used to account for differences in evolutionary patterns among genes. Bootstrap analysis will help assess the consistency of inferred branches under resampling of the sequence alignment.

### 4. Research Rationale

Although mitochondrial genes are frequently used for molecular phylogenetics, their usefulness varies according to the evolutionary depth being investigated, the organisms selected, and the analytical methods employed. Closely related species may possess highly similar mitochondrial protein sequences, whereas more distantly related species may exhibit greater sequence divergence. These differences can influence the resolution and statistical support of inferred phylogenetic trees.

Furthermore, the trees inferred from individual genes may differ in their resolution or branching patterns. Such differences can result from limited informative sites, variation in evolutionary rates, alignment uncertainty, model assumptions, or other biological and analytical factors. Comparing individual gene trees with a tree reconstructed from the combined mitochondrial protein dataset can therefore provide insights into the distribution of phylogenetic information across the mitochondrial genome.

It is also important to recognize that the 13 mitochondrial protein-coding genes are physically linked within the same mitochondrial genome and generally share a common maternal inheritance history in mammals. Therefore, they should not be treated as 13 fully independent estimates of the species tree. The resulting phylogeny primarily represents mitochondrial evolutionary history, which may not always correspond perfectly to the evolutionary history inferred from nuclear genomic data.

### 5. Aim of the Project

The primary aim of this project is to reconstruct and compare the evolutionary relationships among selected mammalian species using all 13 mitochondrial protein-coding genes through computational phylogenetic analysis.

### 6. Objectives

The specific objectives are:

1. To retrieve and curate mitochondrial genome sequences from selected mammalian species using publicly available databases.
2. To extract and validate the 13 mitochondrial protein-coding genes from each selected species.
3. To perform multiple sequence alignment for each protein and evaluate sequence conservation and variation.
4. To construct individual gene phylogenies and investigate differences in their evolutionary resolution.
5. To generate a concatenated protein sequence dataset and reconstruct a combined mitochondrial phylogeny.
6. To compare distance-based and Maximum Likelihood methods for phylogenetic inference.
7. To assess branch support using bootstrap analysis and compare the stability of inferred relationships.
8. To interpret the results in the context of known mammalian evolutionary relationships and discuss the limitations of mitochondrial phylogenies.

### 7. Expected Outcomes

The project is expected to produce a comparative computational framework for investigating mammalian mitochondrial evolution. The principal outputs will include curated sequence datasets, multiple sequence alignments, individual gene trees, a concatenated mitochondrial protein phylogeny, evolutionary distance matrices, and statistical assessments of branch support.

The comparison of individual and concatenated gene analyses is expected to reveal how the amount and distribution of sequence information influence phylogenetic resolution. Differences among analytical methods will also help demonstrate the importance of model assumptions and statistical validation in evolutionary inference.

Ultimately, this project aims to provide a reproducible bioinformatics workflow that integrates sequence retrieval, data curation, multiple sequence alignment, phylogenetic reconstruction, and evolutionary interpretation. The findings will contribute to an improved understanding of the strengths and limitations of mitochondrial protein-coding genes in reconstructing mammalian evolutionary relationships.

### 8. Scope and Limitations

This study focuses on the 13 protein-coding genes of the mitochondrial genome in a selected set of mammalian species. Its conclusions will therefore be limited by taxon sampling, sequence quality, alignment accuracy, evolutionary model selection, and the amount of informative variation present in the selected genes.

Because mitochondrial genes are linked and generally share a common inheritance history, the combined mitochondrial phylogeny should not be interpreted as definitive evidence of the complete species evolutionary history. Broader conclusions about mammalian evolution would benefit from comparison with independent nuclear genes, genomic datasets, fossil evidence, and established evolutionary studies.

The project is intended as a computational investigation of molecular evolution rather than a definitive reconstruction of the entire mammalian evolutionary tree.
